# Setup — 5 minutes

1. Copy these 5 items into your `absalamofficial-cmyk.github.io` repo root,
   keeping the folder structure:
   - `index.html`
   - `apps.json`
   - `scraper.py`
   - `requirements.txt`
   - `.github/workflows/update-apps.yml`

2. Commit and push. GitHub Pages will serve `index.html` as before —
   nothing else changes about hosting.

3. On GitHub → your repo → **Actions** tab → you should see
   "Update Play Store data". Click **Run workflow** once manually to do
   the first refresh (after that it runs automatically every 6 hours).

4. That's it. `apps.json` will now update itself every 6 hours with the
   real name, icon, star rating, review count, and install tier straight
   from each app's public Play Store page — no manual edits needed.

## Adding a new app later

Open `scraper.py`, add one line to the `PACKAGE_IDS` dictionary near the
top with the new package id, its category tag, and a one-line description:

```python
"com.your.newapp": ("Category Tag", "One-line description.", "Display Name"),
```

Commit that one line. On the next scheduled run (or click **Run workflow**
to do it immediately) the new app's real name/icon/rating/downloads get
pulled in and it appears on the site in the exact same card format as the
others — no HTML editing required.

## Why this is the "real" data ceiling

Google Play does not publish an exact install counter to anyone —
not to visitors of your website, and not even to you in a public API.
The only install figures Play Store shows publicly are rounded tiers
(`10+`, `50+`, `100+`, `500+`, `1,000+` …), which is exactly what
`scraper.py` reads and what the site displays. That's the same number a
shopper sees on the actual Play Store page — nothing here is fake or
estimated, but it also won't ever show an exact "1,247 installs" figure,
because that number isn't public anywhere.

If you eventually want your *exact* internal install/uninstall counts
(the ones visible only inside Play Console) surfaced on the site, that
needs the official Google Play Developer Reporting API with a service
account key generated from your own Play Console — a bigger, separate
setup involving Google Cloud credentials that only you (the account
owner) can create. Happy to help wire that up too if you want to go that
route later.
